---
---

<section id="strategy-section" class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 mb-12">
  <div class="vercel-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 border border-zinc-800/80 flex flex-col gap-6">
    
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 border-b border-zinc-800 pb-4">
      <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[#00e5ff]">Grandmaster Playbook</span>
      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white">
        Pro Strategies & Tactical Principles
      </h2>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
        Mastering hexagonal geometry requires spatial awareness and understanding the trade-off between board growth and aggressive leaps.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs text-zinc-300">
      
      <!-- Tip 1: Edge & Corner Anchoring -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-950/60 border border-zinc-800/80 flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-800 text-zinc-300 font-mono text-[10px]">Defense</span>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-white">Edge & Corner Anchoring</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          Hexes on outer perimeters have only 3 to 4 neighbors compared to 6 in the interior. Anchoring your chain along edges prevents the enemy from completely surrounding and converting your clusters.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tip 2: The Jump Trap -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-950/60 border border-zinc-800/80 flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-300 border border-amber-800 font-mono text-[10px]">Caution</span>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-white">Beware the Reckless Jump</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          Leaping into open enemy territory might capture 2 pieces, but leaves an isolated token exposed to counter-infections. Prefer cloning over jumping unless the capture yields a 3+ piece swing or seals an endgame cluster.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tip 3: Denial & Bottleneck Control -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-950/60 border border-zinc-800/80 flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-rose-950/80 text-rose-300 border border-rose-800 font-mono text-[10px]">Control</span>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-white">Choke Points & Donut Rings</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          In maps like The Donut Void or Quantum Hourglass, controlling the narrow bridges cuts off the opponent's expansion, forcing them to pass turns or jump blindly into your territory.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tip 4: Late Game Sweep -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-950/60 border border-zinc-800/80 flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-emerald-950/80 text-emerald-300 border border-emerald-800 font-mono text-[10px]">Endgame</span>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-white">Chain Reaction Conversions</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          When the board is over 80% full, single moves can convert up to 6 adjacent hexes in one turn. Calculate multiple moves ahead to ensure your conversion cannot be immediately recaptured on the next turn.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>

  </div>
</section>
